/*
[bifurcation]
parameter: Da
min: 0.015
max: 0.12
ds: 0.0005
dsMax: 0.004
maxSteps: 8000
*/

// NON-ISOTHERMAL CSTR - the classic ignition/extinction switch.
//
//   Run:  parameter Da, range 0.015 : 0.12, plot x
//   Then: Curve = Fold curve, second parameter B
//
// A first-order exothermic reaction in a cooled stirred tank, in the usual
// dimensionless form:
//
//   dx/dt = -x + Da (1-x) exp(y / (1 + y/gam))
//   dy/dt = -(1+bet) y + B Da (1-x) exp(y / (1 + y/gam))
//
// x is conversion, y is temperature rise. Da is the Damkoehler number
// (residence time), B the heat of reaction, bet the cooling, gam the
// activation energy.
//
// At B = 8 with no cooling the two folds sit at Da = 0.02935 and 0.05733.
//
// The S-shaped curve is the ignition/extinction hysteresis every reactor
// engineer knows: turn the residence time up slowly and the reactor sits
// cold until it ignites at the upper fold; turn it back down and it stays
// hot past the point where it ignited, until it extinguishes at the lower
// fold. The width of that hysteresis is the gap between the two folds.
//
// Continuing the folds in (Da, B) finds the CUSP where the two of them
// merge - the heat of reaction below which the reactor cannot be made to
// ignite at all. That is the number the design question actually turns on,
// and it is a codimension-2 quantity: no single one-parameter diagram
// shows it.

-> x; -x + Da*(1 - x)*exp(y/(1 + y/gam))
-> y; -(1 + bet)*y + B*Da*(1 - x)*exp(y/(1 + y/gam))

Da  = 0.04
B   = 8
bet = 0
gam = 20

x = 0.02
y = 0.05
